Rework the previous VITALIS landing page.

The current visual direction is good, but I want to make the 3D scrolling experience much more cinematic and memorable.

CORE IDEA:

Do not rely entirely on abstract particles.

Use PARTICLE-TO-IMAGE MORPHING as the central visual language.

The website should feel like biomedical data physically transforming into meaningful structures.

The visual sequence should communicate:

DATA
→
HUMAN
→
BIOLOGY
→
COMPUTATION
→
INTELLIGENCE

IMPORTANT:

The 3D scene should remain persistent throughout the page.

Scrolling should transform the same visual system rather than switching between unrelated sections.

VISUAL TRANSFORMATION

STATE 01 — RAW DATA

Begin with thousands of small particles floating in a dark environment.

The particles are irregular and disorganized.

They subtly react to the cursor.

The hero text is large and clearly readable.

Display:

VITALIS

HYBRID INTELLIGENCE
FOR EARLY DISEASE SCREENING

Supporting text:

"Where biomedical data meets classical and quantum computation."

CTA:

ENTER VITALIS

Secondary CTA:

EXPLORE RESEARCH


STATE 02 — HUMAN FORM

As the user scrolls:

The particles begin moving toward one another.

They gradually form an abstract HUMAN SILHOUETTE.

The silhouette should be constructed entirely from particles.

Do not use a photographic human.

Do not make it creepy or anatomical.

It should look like a human-shaped data structure.

Small particles should continue moving around the silhouette.

Text:

"Every patient is more than a dataset."


STATE 03 — BIOLOGICAL STRUCTURE

Continue scrolling.

The human silhouette begins dissolving.

Particles from the torso and cardiovascular region reorganize into an abstract cardiovascular structure.

The transformation should feel continuous:

HUMAN SILHOUETTE
→
PARTICLES
→
CARDIOVASCULAR STRUCTURE

Do not suddenly replace one image with another.

The user should feel that the first structure is transforming into the second.

Display:

CARDIOVASCULAR

6 FEATURES
5 MODELS


STATE 04 — IMAGE TRANSFORMATION

Allow the cardiovascular visualization to transition into a more detailed scientific medical visualization.

Use a high-quality scientific/anatomical visual treatment rather than generic hospital imagery.

The image should partially dissolve back into particles.

The particles then reorganize into another biological pattern representing the breast-cancer module.

Display:

BREAST CANCER

30 FEATURES
5 MODELS


STATE 05 — DATA REASSEMBLES

The biological visualization dissolves.

Particles spread throughout the screen.

They begin organizing into a mathematical/computational structure.

This structure should feel like data being processed.

Text:

"Biological complexity becomes computational structure."


STATE 06 — CLASSICAL + QUANTUM

The computational structure separates into two pathways.

CLASSICAL

Logistic Regression
Random Forest
RBF SVM

QUANTUM

QSVM
VQC

The same particles travel through both pathways.

Do NOT visually imply that quantum is superior.

Display:

TWO COMPUTATIONAL PARADIGMS

ONE EVALUATION FRAMEWORK


STATE 07 — CONVERGENCE

Both pathways converge.

Particles from both systems merge into one central structure.

Reveal:

PREDICTION
CONSENSUS
EXPLAINABILITY
BENCHMARKING

Headline:

"Different models.
One screening workflow."


STATE 08 — EXPLAINABILITY

The central structure breaks apart.

Individual particles/signals become visible.

Some signals are highlighted.

Display:

FEATURE
MODEL INFLUENCE
LOCAL SENSITIVITY

Headline:

"PREDICTION SHOULD BE INSPECTABLE."


STATE 09 — RESEARCH

Camera pulls back dramatically.

All the previous visual structures become part of one larger computational environment.

Display:

RESEARCH QUESTION

"Can quantum-kernel and variational quantum approaches provide competitive performance against established classical ML models under identical evaluation conditions?"

Below this, use smaller technical metadata:

DATASET
FEATURE ENGINEERING
MODEL ARCHITECTURE
EVALUATION
REPRODUCIBILITY


STATE 10 — FINAL

All visual structures slowly dissolve.

Particles converge into one small point.

The point expands.

VITALIS appears.

Text:

EXPLORE THE INTELLIGENCE
BEHIND THE PREDICTION.

CTA:

ENTER SCREENING


TYPOGRAPHY — VERY IMPORTANT

Do NOT make all text small.

Create a strict typography hierarchy.

HERO WORDMARK:

VITALIS
Very large but not oversized to the point of covering the entire screen.

PRIMARY HEADLINES:

Use large editorial typography.

Approximate desktop scale:
56–88 px depending on viewport.

Examples:

HYBRID INTELLIGENCE
FOR EARLY DISEASE SCREENING

"Every patient is more than a dataset."

"Biological complexity becomes computational structure."

"Different models.
One screening workflow."

"Prediction should be inspectable."

These should be visually dominant.

SECONDARY TEXT:

18–24 px.

Use for:
- explanations
- descriptions
- supporting statements

TECHNICAL LABELS:

11–14 px.

Use for:
- CLASSICAL
- QUANTUM
- DATASET
- FEATURES
- MODELS
- EVALUATION
- REPRODUCIBILITY

Do NOT make technical labels compete with the main headline.

BUTTONS:

14–16 px.

NAVIGATION:

13–15 px.

BENCHMARK NUMBERS:

Large enough to be immediately readable.

Use approximately:
32–56 px for major metrics.

TYPOGRAPHIC PRINCIPLE:

The hierarchy should be:

1. MAIN HEADLINE
2. PRIMARY VISUAL
3. SUPPORTING STATEMENT
4. CTA
5. TECHNICAL INFORMATION

Do not make every piece of text equally prominent.

Do not shrink typography simply to create a "minimal" aesthetic.

Use large typography combined with large negative space.

LAYOUT

Avoid a conventional card-grid SaaS layout.

Use:

- asymmetric compositions
- full-screen visual environments
- large negative space
- text anchored to different regions of the viewport
- occasional overlap between text and 3D
- full-width scientific visualizations
- editorial composition

The 3D visualization should occupy approximately 60–80% of the visual attention in important sections.

The text should guide the user through the visualization rather than compete with it.


IMAGE STYLE

When using image-based visual states:

Prefer:
- scientific visualization
- high-quality anatomical rendering
- monochrome or restrained color treatment
- transparent/isolated subjects
- imagery that can plausibly be converted into a particle representation

Avoid:
- stock medical photographs
- doctors
- hospitals
- generic healthcare imagery
- cheesy medical illustrations
- overly colorful anatomy diagrams

The images should feel integrated into the computational environment.


MORPHING PRINCIPLE

The most important interaction is:

IMAGE → PARTICLES → NEW IMAGE

Never simply fade between unrelated images.

The particles should physically carry the transition.

For example:

Human silhouette
        ↓
silhouette dissolves
        ↓
particles scatter
        ↓
particles flow toward cardiovascular structure
        ↓
cardiovascular structure forms

This should feel like the SAME data transforming.

SCROLLING

Scrolling controls:

- particle position
- particle morph targets
- camera position
- camera rotation
- object scale
- image opacity
- particle density
- lighting
- text position
- text opacity

Use long scroll sections with sticky visual scenes.

The user should feel like they are moving through a continuous 3D simulation.

VISUAL STYLE

Premium scientific visualization.

Dark.
Cinematic.
Precise.
Human-designed.

Not:
generic AI startup
cyberpunk
crypto
medical SaaS
template dashboard

Avoid excessive:
- neon
- glow
- glass cards
- rounded containers
- gradients
- decorative icons
- floating holograms

The 3D transformation is the spectacle.

The typography and interface should provide restraint and credibility.

FINAL IMPRESSION:

The user should remember the experience as:

"I scrolled through a living dataset that transformed into a human, then biological structures, then computational systems."

Not:

"I visited another AI website."